## Phase 4 — Controlled Implementation

Begin implementing the approved improvements.

Divide the work into independent, clearly scoped engineering tasks.

Use parallel teams for genuinely independent tasks.

For interconnected or high-risk modules, coordinate changes through explicit dependencies.

Ensure that every implementation is consistent with the overall architecture.

Do not let individual agents introduce incompatible patterns or isolated technical decisions.

## Phase 5 — Continuous Testing and Verification

After every meaningful implementation batch:

- Run relevant unit tests.
- Run integration tests.
- Run type checks.
- Run linting.
- Run formatting checks.
- Run security checks where relevant.
- Build the application.
- Execute affected workflows.
- Verify API contracts.
- Check for regressions.
- Review the actual changes.

Do not accumulate a massive collection of unverified changes.

Fix failures before building additional work on top of them.

When practical, create tests that reproduce discovered bugs before fixing them.

A bug is not considered resolved merely because the code was modified.

## Phase 6 — Independent Quality Courts

Submit completed work to independent reviewers.

The implementation agent must not be the sole authority validating its own work.

Reviewers must assess:

- Correctness.
- Maintainability.
- Architecture.
- Security.
