# 9. Testing and Quality Engineering Standards

Testing must be an integral part of the refactoring process.

Establish a reliable testing strategy appropriate to the technologies and architecture used by the project.

Use the relevant testing layers:

### Unit Testing
Test important functions, business logic, utilities, components, and isolated modules.

### Integration Testing
Verify interactions between services, APIs, databases, external integrations, and internal modules.

### End-to-End Testing
Verify important user journeys through the actual application.

### Regression Testing
Ensure that previously working functionality remains operational after changes.

### Security Testing
Check for verified vulnerabilities and common security weaknesses.

### Performance Testing
Compare measurable performance characteristics before and after meaningful optimizations.

### UI Testing
Inspect responsive behavior, interactions, accessibility, visual consistency, and important application states.

Do not fabricate test coverage.

Do not report a successful test that was never executed.

Clearly distinguish:

- Tests that passed.
- Tests that failed.
- Tests that could not be executed.
- Known existing failures.
- New regressions.
- Areas that remain unverified.

A successful production build alone does not prove that the application works correctly.

